### 1.1 Motivation: Tesla’s Unfinished Symphony

At the beginning of the twentieth century, Nikola Tesla imagined a “world system” that would transmit power and intelligence through a single medium—the earth and its atmosphere. His Wardenclyffe Tower was meant to be the first node of a planetary network in which electrical energy and messages would travel together, abolishing the clutter of separate wires and the tyranny of isolated generation. Although the tower was never completed, Tesla’s insight has proved prophetic: in any complex technological system, energy and information are not independent resources. Their flows are deeply intertwined, and treating them as separable leads to hidden inefficiencies, fragility, and an inability to adapt to changing conditions.

A century later, we have built two enormous, globe‑spanning networks: the electrical grid and the internet. Each is a marvel of engineering, yet their interaction remains an afterthought. Smart grids overlay communication protocols onto a power system that was designed long before the digital age; the resulting architecture is a loose coupling in which the physical limits of information transmission are rarely considered in power‑system control, while energy constraints are typically ignored in the design of network protocols. This artificial separation forces system operators to accept a permanent trade‑off: either they sacrifice responsiveness to save communication energy, or they burn extra power to keep data flowing—without a principled way to choose the optimal balance.

Recent technological trends are finally making Tesla’s unified vision feasible. Simultaneous Wireless Information and Power Transfer (SWIPT) allows a single radio‑frequency signal to carry both data and harvestable energy; a receiver can split the incoming waveform between its information decoder and its rectifier circuit. Integrated Sensing, Communication, and Powering (ISCAP), a cornerstone of emerging 6G standards, extends this idea to a single air interface that simultaneously senses the environment, exchanges data, and delivers power. At the same time, the proliferation of distributed energy resources—rooftop solar, batteries, electric vehicles—and the rise of edge intelligence are creating a pressing need for decentralized control strategies that can react to local conditions without relying on a perfect, delay‑free communication backbone.

What has been missing is a rigorous mathematical framework that captures the bidirectional coupling between energy and information at the network level—a framework that can guarantee stability, optimize efficiency, and remain robust under uncertainty. This thesis develops that framework.

