### 1.3 Mathematical Preliminaries from First Principles

A central goal of this thesis is to build a rigorous bridge between three traditionally separate mathematical domains: algebraic graph theory, nonlinear dynamical systems, and information theory/stochastic thermodynamics. This section introduces, from first principles, the essential concepts and notation that will be used throughout the work. The reader familiar with these topics may skim this section and refer back as needed.

#### 1.3.1 Graph Theory and the Laplacian

A *weighted directed graph* \(\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}, \mathbf{W})\) consists of a finite vertex set \(\mathcal{V}=\{1,\dots,N\}\), an edge set \(\mathcal{E}\subseteq \mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\), and a non‑negative weight matrix \(\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{N\times N}\) with the property that \(\mathbf{W}_{ij}>0\) iff \((j,i)\in\mathcal{E}\). For any node \(i\), the weighted out‑degree and in‑degree are \(d_i^{\text{out}} = \sum_{j} \mathbf{W}_{ji}\) and \(d_i^{\text{in}} = \sum_{j} \mathbf{W}_{ij}\) (note the transposition convention). Throughout this thesis we restrict attention to **balanced graphs**, i.e., graphs where \(d_i^{\text{out}} = d_i^{\text{in}}\) for every \(i\); all undirected graphs (where \(\mathbf{W}=\mathbf{W}^\top\)) are trivially balanced.

The **Laplacian matrix** associated with \(\mathcal{G}\) is defined as

\[
\mathbf{L} = \mathbf{D} - \mathbf{W},
\tag{1.5}
\]

where \(\mathbf{D} = \operatorname{diag}(d_1^{\text{out}})\) (or equivalently \(d_i^{\text{in}}\) for balanced graphs). By construction, \(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}=0\), where \(\mathbf{1}\) is the vector of all ones. For an undirected graph, \(\mathbf{L}\) is symmetric positive semidefinite; its eigenvalues can be ordered as

\[
0 = \lambda_1 \le \lambda_2 \le \cdots \le \lambda_N.
\]

The second smallest eigenvalue \(\lambda_2\) is called the **algebraic connectivity** (or Fiedler value) and is strictly positive if and only if the graph is connected. It quantifies the speed of diffusion processes on the graph: for the linear consensus protocol \(\dot{\mathbf{x}} = -\mathbf{L}\mathbf{x}\), the deviation from the average decays as \(e^{-\lambda_2 t}\).

When the edge weights are time‑varying—as they will be in the EIUN—we obtain a time‑varying Laplacian \(\mathbf{L}(t)\). A persistent connectivity condition (Chapter 4) will guarantee that \(\lambda_2(\mathbf{L}(t)) \ge \lambda_{\min}>0\) uniformly in \(t\), which is the key to proving exponential stability.

#### 1.3.2 Dynamical Systems and Stability

We model the network as an **ordinary differential equation** (ODE) on a state space \(\mathbb{R}^n\):

\[
\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, t), \qquad \mathbf{x}(0) = \mathbf{x}_0,
\tag{1.6}
\]

where \(\mathbf{f}\) is piecewise continuous in \(t\) and locally Lipschitz in \(\mathbf{x}\) to guarantee existence and uniqueness of solutions. An **equilibrium point** \(\mathbf{x}^*\) satisfies \(\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}^*,t) \equiv 0\) for all \(t\). The main stability notions used in this work are:

* **Exponential stability:** There exist constants \(c,\alpha >0\) such that \(\|\mathbf{x}(t)-\mathbf{x}^*\| \le c e^{-\alpha t}\|\mathbf{x}(0)-\mathbf{x}^*\|\).
* **Input‑to‑state stability (ISS):** For any bounded disturbance \(\mathbf{d}(t)\), the state satisfies \(\|\mathbf{x}(t)-\mathbf{x}^*\| \le \beta(\|\mathbf{x}(0)-\mathbf{x}^*\|,t) + \gamma(\|\mathbf{d}\|_\infty)\), where \(\beta\) is a class‑\(\mathcal{KL}\) function and \(\gamma\) is a class‑\(\mathcal{K}\) function. In this thesis we will prove exponential ISS, i.e., \(\beta(r,t) = c e^{-\alpha t} r\) and \(\gamma(r) = c_\gamma r\).

When the equilibrium is not isolated but forms a manifold \(\mathcal{E}\), we adapt the ISS concept to **set‑wise ISS**, measuring the distance to the set \(\operatorname{dist}(\mathbf{x},\mathcal{E}) = \inf_{\mathbf{y}\in\mathcal{E}} \|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}\|\).

The principal tool for proving stability is Lyapunov’s direct method. We construct a scalar function \(V(\mathbf{x})\ge 0\) that vanishes on the equilibrium set and has a negative‑definite derivative along trajectories. A classical choice for consensus networks is \(V = \frac{1}{2}\|\mathbf{x}-\bar{x}\mathbf{1}\|^2\), which yields \(\dot{V} \le -\lambda_2 V\) when \(\dot{\mathbf{x}} = -\mathbf{L}\mathbf{x}\).

#### 1.3.3 Information‑Theoretic Quantities

Let \(\Omega\) be a measurable space (the environment). A **belief** is a probability measure \(\mu\) on \(\Omega\). For two probability measures \(\mu, \nu\) on \(\Omega\) with \(\mu\) absolutely continuous with respect to \(\nu\), the **Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence** is

\[
D_{\text{KL}}(\mu \| \nu) = \int_\Omega \log\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}\, d\mu.
\tag{1.7}
\]

It is non‑negative and vanishes iff \(\mu=\nu\) almost everywhere. The KL divergence plays a central role in the thermodynamic efficiency bound of Theorem 4.

For random variables \(X\) and \(Y\) with joint distribution \(P_{XY}\) and marginals \(P_X, P_Y\), the **mutual information** is

\[
I(X;Y) = D_{\text{KL}}(P_{XY} \| P_X \otimes P_Y).
\tag{1.8}
\]

In the context of an agent interacting with a stochastic environment, the **predictive information** \(I_{\text{pred}} = I(\text{state}; \text{future environment})\) quantifies how much the agent’s internal state reduces uncertainty about upcoming observations. The **memory information** \(I_{\text{mem}} = I(\text{state}; \text{past environment})\) measures how much of the past is retained. The difference \(I_{\text{mem}} - I_{\text{pred}}\) appears in the thermodynamic bound of Quenneville and Sivak (see §3.3).

#### 1.3.4 Wasserstein Distance and Barycenters

Let \(\mathcal{P}_2(\Omega)\) be the space of probability measures on \(\Omega\subseteq\mathbb{R}^d\) with finite second moment. The **2‑Wasserstein distance** between \(\mu,\nu\in\mathcal{P}_2(\Omega)\) is

\[
W_2(\mu,\nu) = \left( \inf_{\gamma \in \Pi(\mu,\nu)} \int_{\Omega\times\Omega} \|x-y\|^2 \, d\gamma(x,y) \right)^{1/2},
\tag{1.9}
\]

where \(\Pi(\mu,\nu)\) is the set of all joint distributions with marginals \(\mu\) and \(\nu\). Unlike the KL divergence, \(W_2\) is a true metric that respects the geometry of the underlying space. For Gaussian distributions \(\mu=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{m}_\mu,\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\mu)\), \(\nu=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{m}_\nu,\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\nu)\), the distance has an analytic form:

\[
W_2^2(\mu,\nu) = \|\mathbf{m}_\mu-\mathbf{m}_\nu\|^2 + \operatorname{Tr}\!\Bigl(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\mu + \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\nu - 2\bigl(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\mu^{1/2}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\nu\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_\mu^{1/2}\bigr)^{1/2}\Bigr).
\tag{1.10}
\]

Given a set of measures \(\mu_1,\dots,\mu_k\) and non‑negative weights \(\lambda_\ell\) summing to \(1\), their **Wasserstein barycenter** is the minimiser

\[
\bar{\mu} = \arg\min_{\mu\in\mathcal{P}_2(\Omega)} \sum_{\ell=1}^k \lambda_\ell\, W_2^2(\mu, \mu_\ell).
\tag{1.11}
\]

For Gaussians, the barycenter is another Gaussian whose mean is the weighted average of the means and whose covariance is the unique positive definite solution of a matrix equation (see Chapter 5). This makes the belief update computationally tractable.

#### 1.3.5 Thermodynamic Dissipation Bounds

The second law of thermodynamics links information processing to energy dissipation. The classical Landauer principle states that erasing one bit of information in a memory at temperature \(T\) dissipates at least \(k_B T \ln 2\) of heat. For nonequilibrium systems, a more general bound exists. Consider a system coupled to a thermal bath at inverse temperature \(\beta = 1/(k_B T)\) and exchanging energy and information with its environment over a time interval \([0,\tau]\). Let \(\sigma\) be the total entropy production and \(W_{\text{diss}} = k_B T\sigma\) the dissipated work. Under Markovian and quasi‑static assumptions, Quenneville and Sivak (2018) proved

\[
\beta W_{\text{diss}} \ge I_{\text{mem}} - I_{\text{pred}},
\tag{1.12}
\]

where \(I_{\text{mem}} = I(\text{state}_{[0,\tau]}; \text{past environment})\) and \(I_{\text{pred}} = I(\text{state}_{[0,\tau]}; \text{future environment})\). This inequality tells us that dissipation can be reduced by making the internal state more predictive of the future—i.e., by improving the agent’s model of the environment. In the EIUN, the epistemic layer maintains a belief \(b_i(t)\) that directly influences \(I_{\text{pred},i}\), so (1.12) provides a fundamental performance limit for the whole network.

