**Appendix E**  
**Proof of Theorem 5.2: Byzantine‑Resilient Trimmed Wasserstein Consensus**

Assume the communication graph is \((2f+1)\)-vertex‑connected, and at most \(f\) nodes are Byzantine. Each correct node \(i\) receives beliefs from its neighbours, trims the \(f\) farthest in \(W_2\) distance (after computing a reference median), and then takes the barycenter of the remaining set.

Because the graph is \((2f+1)\)-connected, even after removing any \(f\) nodes, the remaining subgraph is connected. Moreover, after trimming \(f\) outliers, each correct node retains at least \(f+1\) correct neighbours (since there are at most \(f\) Byzantine nodes in the neighbourhood). The trimmed set is therefore dominated by correct beliefs.

The trimmed barycenter operator is still non‑expansive with respect to the set of correct nodes. Define the maximum pairwise distance among correct nodes \(D^{(n)}_{\text{cor}}\). The update reduces \(D^{(n)}_{\text{cor}}\) by a factor \(\rho < 1\) as in the fault‑free case, because the Byzantine beliefs are either discarded or, if they coincidentally lie within the convex hull, do not increase the maximum distance. Consequently, the beliefs of all correct nodes converge exponentially to a common measure \(b^\dagger\) that lies in the Wasserstein convex hull of the initial correct beliefs. ∎

