**Appendix G**  
**Proof of Theorem 6.2: Global Efficiency Bound**

We derive the bound \(\beta W_{\text{diss}}^{\text{total}} \ge \sum_i \Delta D_{\text{KL}}^{(i)} + \mathcal{E}_{\text{consensus}}\).

Starting from the single‑node bound (Theorem 3.1), for each node \(i\),

\[
\beta \int_0^T \dot{W}_{\text{diss},i} \, dt \ge D_{\text{KL}}(b_i(T)\|\pi_i^{\text{ss}}) - D_{\text{KL}}(b_i(0)\|\pi_i^{\text{ss}}) + \mathcal{E}_i,
\]

with \(|\mathcal{E}_i| \le C_\Omega \int_0^T W_2(b_i(t), \pi_i^{\text{ss}})\,dt\). Summing over \(i\),

\[
\beta W_{\text{diss}}^{\text{total}} \ge \sum_i \Delta D_{\text{KL}}^{(i)} + \sum_i \mathcal{E}_i.
\]

Now, let \(\bar{b}(t)\) be the Wasserstein barycenter of all node beliefs at time \(t\) (the consensus belief). By the triangle inequality,

\[
W_2(b_i, \pi_i^{\text{ss}}) \le W_2(b_i, \bar{b}) + W_2(\bar{b}, \pi_i^{\text{ss}}).
\]

The first term is precisely the consensus error that decays exponentially by Theorem 5.1, so \(\int_0^T W_2(b_i, \bar{b})\,dt\) is bounded by the total consensus error \(\mathcal{E}_{\text{consensus},i}\). The second term, \(W_2(\bar{b}, \pi_i^{\text{ss}})\), is the mismatch between the consensus belief and the stationary environment; if the true environment distribution lies within the model class used by the nodes, this term converges to zero as the consensus belief approaches the truth, otherwise it is a constant offset that can be absorbed into the initial KL divergence.

Therefore, \(\sum_i \mathcal{E}_i \ge -C \sum_i \int_0^T W_2(b_i, \bar{b})\,dt - \text{const}\). Defining \(\mathcal{E}_{\text{consensus}}(T) = C_\Omega \sum_i \int_0^T W_2(b_i, \bar{b})\,dt\) (which is non‑negative) gives the final inequality. ∎

