**Appendix I**  
**Numerical Methods for Wasserstein Barycenter**

For general discrete measures, the barycenter is computed by the Sinkhorn algorithm with entropic regularisation.

**I.1 Discrete Barycenter.** Let each belief be represented by a set of \(M\) points in \(\mathbb{R}^d\) with weights \(\mathbf{a}^{(j)}\). The squared Euclidean cost matrix between the points of two measures \(\mu_j, \mu_k\) is \(\mathbf{C}^{jk}\). The entropically regularised barycenter with parameter \(\varepsilon\) minimizes

\[
\min_{\mu} \sum_{j} \lambda_j \Bigl( \min_{\pi^{j}} \langle \mathbf{C}^{j}, \pi^{j} \rangle + \varepsilon \sum_{k,\ell} \pi_{k\ell}^{j} \log \pi_{k\ell}^{j} \Bigr),
\]

subject to \(\pi^{j}\mathbf{1} = \mathbf{a}^{(j)}\) and \(\pi^{j\top}\mathbf{1} = \mathbf{a}\) (the barycenter’s weight vector). This is solved by alternating Sinkhorn projections; see Peyré & Cuturi (2019).

**I.2 Gaussian Barycenter.** For Gaussian beliefs \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{m}_i, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_i)\), the barycenter is also Gaussian with mean \(\bar{\mathbf{m}} = \sum \lambda_i \mathbf{m}_i\). The covariance \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\) satisfies the fixed point equation

\[
\boldsymbol{\Sigma} = \sum_i \lambda_i (\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{1/2} \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_i \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{1/2})^{1/2}.
\]

This can be solved by iterative averaging: \(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{k+1} = \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{1/2} \left( \sum_i \lambda_i (\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{-1/2} \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_i \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{-1/2})^{1/2} \right) \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_k^{1/2}\), converging rapidly (Alvarez‑Esteban et al., 2016).

